CAT = "ukfawBurns"; SEG = "BRN"
Q = [
("For how long should you cool a burn under running water?",
 "At least 20 minutes", ["At least 2 minutes", "At least 5 minutes", "At least 30 seconds"],
 "Current UK advice is to cool a burn under cool or lukewarm running water for at least 20 minutes. Cooling for that long limits how deep the damage goes."),
("What should you use to cool a burn?",
 "Cool or lukewarm water", ["Ice cubes on the skin", "Butter or cooking oil", "A thick layer of cream"],
 "Cool or lukewarm running water is best. Ice can damage the skin further, and fats or creams hold the heat in and have to be cleaned off in hospital."),
("How should you cover a burn on the forearm once it has been cooled?",
 "Cling film laid along it", ["Cling film wrapped round it", "A fluffy cotton pad", "An adhesive plaster"],
 "Lay cling film lengthways along the burn, or use a clean plastic bag on a hand. It keeps the wound clean without sticking."),
("Why should cling film not be wrapped right round a burnt limb?",
 "Swelling can make it tight", ["It will melt onto the skin", "It stops the burn cooling", "It is not clean enough"],
 "Burns swell, and a tight wrap can act like a tourniquet. Laying it along the limb leaves room to swell."),
("Blisters form on a burn. What should you do?",
 "Leave them intact", ["Burst them with a pin", "Peel the loose skin off", "Rub cream into them"],
 "Blisters protect the damaged skin underneath from infection. Never burst them or remove loose skin."),
("Why should you remove rings and watches near a burn quickly?",
 "Before the area swells", ["To cool the metal down", "So the burn can be seen", "To stop them melting"],
 "Burnt tissue swells fast, and a ring or watch can then cut off circulation. Remove them gently before that happens."),
("Burnt clothing is stuck to the skin. What should you do?",
 "Leave it in place", ["Pull it off quickly", "Soak it off with oil", "Cut it off at the skin"],
 "Removing stuck clothing tears the skin. Cool over it and leave it for hospital staff."),
("Which burn should always get medical attention?",
 "One larger than their hand", ["A red patch on a finger", "A small red mark on the arm", "A tiny blister on a toe"],
 "Burns larger than the casualty's hand need medical help, as do all deep burns, burns to the face, hands, feet or genitals, and chemical or electrical burns."),
("Which sign suggests a deep, full-thickness burn?",
 "Charred or white, little pain", ["Red, sore and dry to touch", "Red with small blisters", "Pink, moist and very painful"],
 "Full-thickness burns destroy nerve endings, so the area can look white, waxy or charred and may hurt surprisingly little. They always need hospital treatment."),
("A dry powder chemical has spilled on a worker's arm. What should you do?",
 "Brush it off, then flood it", ["Wipe it with a wet cloth", "Leave it to dry out", "Neutralise it with some vinegar"],
 "Wearing gloves, brush off dry powder first, then flood the area with plenty of running water. Never try to neutralise a chemical yourself."),
("A chemical has splashed into one eye. How should you rinse it?",
 "With the injured eye lowermost", ["With the injured eye uppermost", "By rubbing the eye clear", "With both eyes shut tight"],
 "Tilt the head so the injured eye is lower, and pour clean water from the inner corner outwards. This stops the chemical washing into the other eye."),
("For at least how long should you rinse a chemical splash in the eye?",
 "At least 20 minutes", ["At least 10 seconds", "At least 30 seconds", "At least 1 minute"],
 "NHS advice is to run plenty of clean water over the eye for at least 20 minutes, and to keep rinsing a strong chemical while you wait for help. Get the casualty to hospital."),
("A speck of dust is floating on the white of the eye. What should you try first?",
 "Rinse it with clean water", ["Pick it off with a tissue", "Rub the eye gently", "Lift it with tweezers"],
 "Rinsing with clean water or eyewash usually floats a loose speck away. Never use anything hard or pointed near the eye."),
("A metal fragment is stuck in the coloured part of the eye. What should you do?",
 "Leave it in, get help", ["Lift it out with a tissue", "Rinse it out with force", "Ask them to rub it out"],
 "Anything stuck on or in the eye must be left for a medical professional. Ask the casualty to keep still and cover the eye with a pad."),
("A colleague is in contact with a faulty kettle's electricity. What should you do first?",
 "Switch off at the mains", ["Pull them off by hand", "Throw water over them", "Push them with a metal bar"],
 "Break the contact by switching off at the mains or plug. If you cannot, stand on dry insulating material and push them away with something non-conducting, such as a wooden broom."),
("Why must every electrical burn be checked in hospital?",
 "Damage can be deep inside", ["They are always very painful", "They bleed heavily", "They need stitches"],
 "Electricity can burn tissue deep along its path between the entry and exit points, and can affect the heart, while the skin wound looks small."),
("A casualty is in contact with a fallen high-voltage power line. What should you do?",
 "Keep well back, call 999", ["Lift the line with a stick", "Pull them by their clothing", "Switch off at a wall socket"],
 "High voltage can arc through the air and through wood. Keep everyone well back and wait until the power company confirms it is off."),
("A worker has swallowed a cleaning chemical. What should you not do?",
 "Make them vomit", ["Call 999 or 111", "Find the container", "Keep them calm"],
 "Never make a casualty vomit, because a corrosive chemical burns the throat again on the way up. Get medical advice and keep the container to show the crew."),
("What should go to hospital with a casualty who has swallowed a poison?",
 "The container or label", ["A sample of their blood", "A glass of milk to drink", "Their accident report"],
 "The container or label tells medical staff exactly what was taken. Note how much and when, if you can."),
("A worker has inhaled fumes in an enclosed area. What should you do?",
 "Fresh air, without going in", ["Go straight in and pull them out", "Give them a sugary drink", "Lie them face down inside"],
 "Never enter a space with toxic fumes yourself. Call 999, raise the alarm and, if it is safe, open doors and windows to let in fresh air."),
("Which signs suggest carbon monoxide poisoning?",
 "Headache, dizziness, nausea", ["Rash, itching and swelling", "Fever, cough and chills", "Sharp pain in one knee only"],
 "Carbon monoxide causes headache, dizziness, nausea, breathlessness and confusion. Get the casualty to fresh air and seek help."),
("Which signs suggest opioid poisoning?",
 "Tiny pupils, slow breathing", ["Wide pupils, fast breathing", "Hot, red, dry skin", "Shaking and sweating"],
 "Opioid poisoning causes very small pupils, slow or irregular breathing and drowsiness or unresponsiveness. Call 999 and start CPR if breathing is not normal."),
("A scald is a burn caused by:",
 "Hot liquid or steam", ["Dry heat or flames", "Friction from a rope", "A chemical splash"],
 "Scalds come from wet heat such as hot drinks, water or steam. Burns from flames or hot objects are dry heat burns."),
("Which signs describe a superficial burn?",
 "Red, sore, no blisters", ["White, waxy, painless", "Charred and leathery", "Deep, with large blisters"],
 "A superficial burn affects only the outer layer of skin: it is red, sore and swollen, but does not blister. Cool it as for any burn."),
("Why are large burns dangerous even after cooling?",
 "Fluid loss and infection", ["They always stop breathing", "They raise blood sugar", "They make bones brittle"],
 "Burns let fluid leak from the body, which can lead to shock, and broken skin lets infection in. Large burns need hospital care."),
("A casualty from a fire has soot round the nose and a hoarse voice. What does this suggest?",
 "A burnt airway", ["A broken nose", "Simple tiredness", "A chest infection"],
 "Soot round the mouth or nose, singed nostrils and a hoarse voice suggest the airway has been burnt and may swell. Call 999 at once."),
("A worker is red and sore after hours in strong sun. What should you do?",
 "Shade, then cool the skin", ["Rub oil into the skin", "Wrap them in a blanket", "Burst any blisters at once"],
 "Move them out of the sun, cool the skin with cool water and give sips of water. Seek help if the skin blisters badly or they feel unwell."),
("A worker's chemical-soaked clothing is still on them. What should you do?",
 "Remove it, protect yourself", ["Leave it on until hospital", "Rinse only their face and hands", "Dry it with a hair dryer"],
 "Wearing gloves, remove contaminated clothing so the chemical stops burning the skin, then flood the area with water. Avoid getting it on yourself."),
]
